+  A brief's `Merges with: <ids>` names an *atomic merge group*: bundles that must
+  not land on the base separately (either side declaring it binds both; a group is
+  every bundle connected that way). At a wave boundary the driver does not ready or
+  merge a COMPLETE member while its group cannot all merge: a member is not COMPLETE,
+  or a member has a publish record merge mode would refuse (no recorded PR because
+  its publish failed, say). It checks every member before it merges the first, so a
+  refusal the run can see coming never lands the group in part. A held member's PR
+  stays a draft, one line says what it waits for, and the run goes on. A bundle that
+  depends on a held member is not built this run, and is named with the group. At
+  the boundary where nothing blocks the group any more, it merges back to back,
+  earlier waves first, then by name, each through the same gates as any other PR. A
+  member that ends DISCONTINUED, or is not built, leaves the rest unmerged. The final
+  wave is the exception, as for every PR: its PRs stay drafts, so a group whose last
+  member finishes there, or that sits wholly in it, is not merged by the run. At its
+  end the run names each group it did not merge whole, with why and what to do; for
+  a group nothing blocks, that is "merge its PRs together", in the order given. A run
+  that held a member at a boundary exits 3 when every bundle finished; a group wholly
+  in the final wave was never held, so it does not change the exit code. GitHub
+  cannot merge two PRs at once: if the host refuses a member's merge after another
+  member merged, the run stops as for any refused merge and names which members
+  landed. Three group shapes cannot work in merge mode, and `pdca flow <ids>` refuses
+  them before anything builds (exit 2, with the ways out): a member that depends on
+  another, directly or through other bundles (it could only build after the other
+  merged, which the group forbids); members that `Conflicts with` each other (the
+  later one would be built on a base without the held earlier one, so landing it
+  would need an update across the declared overlap, which can stop the run with the
+  group half landed); and a `Merges with` id that is not one of the run's bundles
+  (the run only merges its own). Make such a group one bundle, or run the batch in
+  `wave_mode = "stack"`, where the field changes nothing: you merge the stack and can
+  take the group together. A `pdca flow --from-csv` sweep holds such a group instead,
+  and a group one of whose members it holds for any other reason, then what depends
+  on them, and drives the rest. A split during the run re-levels only what is left to
+  build, but a group member the run has already built still counts as one of its
+  bundles there. A bundle that `Conflicts with` a held member but is not in its group
+  is not held: it builds on a base without that member, and its merge can stop the
+  run once the group lands. Give it a `Depends on` the group member if it must wait.
